<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ADDED 14 Oct 2026 (security audit).

  WHY THIS EXISTS. Cleartext traffic is blocked by DEFAULT on modern Android,
  but only because of the target SDK - and that is an implicit guarantee, not an
  explicit one. A security audit found the app relied on that default with
  nothing stating the intent. This file states it, which means:

    1. The guarantee is now a fact about this app rather than a side effect of a
       number in build.gradle. Lowering targetSdk, or a library adding its own
       manifest, cannot silently remove it.
    2. It fails at BUILD time if something needs cleartext, rather than at run
       time on one device in a way nobody would notice.

  WHAT IT COSTS. Three endpoints in this app make network calls and all three
  are HTTPS, verified by curl rather than assumed:
    - api.github.com            (updateCheckService.js, release check)
    - nominatim.openstreetmap.org (locationService.js, reverse + forward geocode)

  A local development server over http would be blocked by this on a real
  device. That is the correct trade for an app holding sexual-health data, and
  Capacitor's dev server is only used on the desktop web build anyway.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <!-- Base: HTTPS only, everywhere. No exceptions granted. -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
